{
  "additional_concerns": [
    "The legacy exclusions in candidates node.1bf6918f69c6b37738daeb83, node.4e6a162c35678e9ddf17ad8e, node.ce439b1cf9ed8301fbb68f94 and node.ea4d19630f8cab09065b1909 implement a proposed inference: preserved application processing defeats relief. The circular expressly preserves processing but does not conclusively establish substantive disqualification or a continuing bar after subsequent decisions, withdrawal or resubmission.",
    "Candidate node.4d548f2e09bc36ef51212f64 places legacy applications outside scope. Its OUT_OF_SCOPE result is not a denial of relief. Candidate node.b66b3e831bb98a12db59dffa deliberately decides only substantive conditions.",
    "The specific Effective Date supports commencement on 28 November 2025, but the introductory immediate-effect wording and the event controlling transitional reliance remain interpretive questions. An assessment date does not independently establish an implementation-date rule.",
    "Item (ii) expressly requires home-jurisdiction compliance. Neither item (i)'s supervision condition nor the introductory statement of purpose conclusively establishes an additional, independent home-supervision test for item (ii).",
    "Concrete classification requires applicable home-jurisdiction requirements and relevant SFC guidance on materiality, novelty, complexity and possible local policy implications. These authorities are not supplied; no identifiable dependency IDs can be assigned to them.",
    "The supplied missing dependency IDs lack document mappings. No catalogue match is inferred. Omission of an external specification is a completeness limitation, not proof that any particular additional Boolean condition belongs in the executable rule.",
    "The predicates do not establish notification, filing or post-vetting compliance, legal approval, or the complete underlying approval obligation. Preservation of a particular exception does not validate the entire candidate."
  ],
